WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.560
Treaty Feasibility and Cost Analysis Previous treaties failed because they ran on guilt.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:16.340
Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency. This one runs on greed, which is (regrettably) humanity's only reliable fuel source.

3
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.660
"Getting one hundred and ninety-five countries to agree costs billions and takes forever.".

4
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.060
No. Getting countries to *implement* things costs billions.

5
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.260
Getting them to *sign papers* is surprisingly cheap. Humans love signing papers.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:43.880
It makes them feel important. Your entire diplomatic system runs on the principle that writing your name on a document feels

7
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.120
like accomplishing something. Sometimes it is. Usually it isn't.

8
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:58.780
But the signing part is always affordable. What Treaties Actually Cost non-governmental organization, or N.G.O.

9
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:07.290
campaign budgets are rarely disclosed publicly, but treaty campaigns run on pocket change compared to the thing they create.

10
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:17.820
The Ottawa Treaty banning landmines took fourteen months. One staff member, Jody Williams, grew a coalition to over a thousand

11
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.740
organizations across sixty countries. one hundred and twenty-two states signed.

12
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:34.140
She won the Nobel Peace Prize. One woman with a phone convinced one hundred and twenty-two countries to stop burying explosives

13
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:42.860
in the ground where children walk. Your species is capable of remarkable things when one person gets sufficiently annoyed.

14
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:52.540
The Cluster Munitions Convention took about two years. Seven governments and a coalition of N.G.O.s got ninety-four states

15
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:58.940
to sign. The Arms Trade Treaty took a decade but started with three supporting governments.

16
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:06.480
The Control Arms coalition published fifty plus reports until one hundred and thirty countries signed.

17
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:16.200
The Nuclear Ban Treaty also took a decade. ICAN ran the entire operation with five staff in a Geneva office.

18
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.560
one hundred and twenty-two states voted yes. Another Nobel Prize.

19
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.960
Five people in a Geneva office won a Nobel Prize. You have more people in your accounting department.

20
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.220
Pattern: Total to get a treaty signed: fifteen to fifty million dollars.

21
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.820
Your species spends more than that on Super Bowl ads for beer.

22
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:49.200
Beer. The thing that makes you temporarily stupider. You will spend more money selling stupidity beverages during one sporting

23
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.320
event than it costs to get one hundred and twenty-two countries to agree on anything.

24
00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:01.280
I've been watching your species for eighty years and the budget comparisons still surprise me.

25
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:11.620
Inflation-Adjusted to twenty twenty-four Dollars Using Bureau of Labor Statistics, or B.L.S., Consumer Price Index, or C.P.I.

26
00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:19.880
data: The Ottawa Treaty of nineteen ninety-seven had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five million dollars,

27
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:27.780
which is thirty to fifty million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of approximately two point zero

28
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:35.640
times. The Cluster Munitions Convention of two thousand and eight had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five

29
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:42.740
million dollars, which is twenty-three to thirty-eight million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of

30
00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:51.140
approximately one point five times. The Arms Trade Treaty of twenty thirteen had an original cost estimate of twenty to

31
00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:58.420
thirty million dollars, which is twenty-seven to forty-one million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier

32
00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:06.800
of approximately one point three five times. The Nuclear Ban Treaty of twenty seventeen had an original cost estimate of

33
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:14.460
ten to twenty million dollars, which is thirteen to twenty-five million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier

34
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:23.380
of approximately one point two five times. In today's money: Treaty campaigns cost roughly twenty-five to fifty million

35
00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:32.220
dollars (twenty twenty-four U.S.D.). Banning landmines: approximately forty million dollars to get the signatures, five

36
00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.560
billion plus dollars to actually dig them up. The paperwork is the cheap part.

37
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:46.440
The paperwork is always the cheap part. The expensive part is doing the thing you wrote on the paperwork, which your species

38
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:55.900
often skips (see: every New Year's resolution in history). Cost for a one percent Health Treaty This one touches military

39
00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:02.840
budgets. Suggest moving one percent of the explosion money and politicians look at you like you've asked to borrow their

40
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:10.780
favorite child. The reaction is disproportionate to the request, but proportionate to the lobbying dollars behind the status

41
00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:19.840
quo. So budget three to four times what landmines cost. Phase one is Pre-negotiations, which includes coalition building

42
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:27.740
and expert drafts. It lasts eighteen to twenty-four months and costs between eight and twelve million dollars, funded by

43
00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.000
philanthropy like Gates or Open Philanthropy and champion governments.

44
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:40.120
Phase two is the Global Campaign, covering the platform, three point five percent consent, and media.

45
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:48.140
It lasts three to five years and costs forty-five to eighty million dollars, funded by philanthropy and crowd-funding.

46
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.100
Phase three is the Diplomatic Process, including conferences and delegate travel.

47
00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:02.340
It lasts eighteen to thirty months and costs fifteen to twenty-five million dollars, funded by host governments and the

48
00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:09.520
U.N. Trust Fund. Phase four is the Ratification Drive, for legal reviews and parliamentary aid.

49
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.740
It lasts two to four years and costs five to eight million dollars, funded by philanthropy.

50
00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:25.920
Phase five is Contingency for opposition management. It is ongoing and costs ten to fifteen million dollars, funded by strategic

51
00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:32.460
communications and lobbying. Total: eighty-three to one hundred and forty million dollars.

52
00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:38.140
The Math. Signing the treaty: approximately eighty-three million dollars.

53
00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.460
What the treaty unlocks: twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year.

54
00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.920
Forever. That's over a three hundred to one return on paperwork.

55
00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:56.680
If paperwork were a stock, this would be the best-performing stock in the history of your financial markets.

56
00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:03.880
It isn't a stock. It's a treaty. But the return is real, which is more than your financial markets can say about most of

57
00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:12.900
their stocks. How to Make Governments Sign Things. Politicians move when they see voters demanding it and political cover

58
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:21.760
to do it. Give them both. First, prove public demand. Get three point five percent of the population (two hundred and eighty

59
00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:30.620
million people) to say "yes" with verified identity. Every nonviolent campaign with three point five percent plus participation

60
00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:37.140
has succeeded. That's not activism. That's a mandate. Second, prove it's safe.

61
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.380
Peer-reviewed modeling showing every nation gains more in health savings than it loses in military spending.

62
00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.620
Politicians need cover the way vampires need darkness. They won't move without it.

63
00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:56.800
But give them the numbers, and they'll move and pretend it was their idea, which is the second most reliable pattern in

64
00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:02.960
your species' political history. (The first most reliable pattern is blaming the previous administration.).

65
00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:13.440
Timeline to "Governments Can't Ignore This". With approximately sixty million dollars in seed funding, you can reach "Treaty-Ready"

66
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:21.400
status in two to three years: a platform with two hundred and eighty million verified supporters (the three point five percent

67
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:29.740
threshold), country-by-country impact modeling, and a coalition of one hundred and twenty plus supportive N.G.O.s and five

68
00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:37.179
to ten champion governments. Once these assets exist, formal negotiations become very hard to avoid.

69
00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:44.660
Not because politicians want them. Because ignoring two hundred and eighty million people is the kind of career decision

70
00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.610
that leads to "spending more time with family.". You aren't buying a treaty.

71
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.680
You're buying the machinery that makes the treaty very hard to refuse.

72
00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:03.840
Political Success Probability: Economic Analysis. This section documents the rationale for the one percent central estimate

73
00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:10.880
(and a twenty-five percent optimistic scenario ceiling). The model assumes a ninety-nine percent chance of failure.

74
00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:17.500
It's still a good bet. On Wishonia, we would not invest in something with a ninety-nine percent chance of failure.

75
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.120
On Earth, this is called "venture capital" and your richest humans do it professionally.

76
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:33.880
Even at one percent probability of success, this intervention's expected cost per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y.,

77
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:43.420
(zero point one seven seven dollars) is still five hundred and three times more cost-effective than malaria bed nets (eighty-nine

78
00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.760
dollars per D.A.L.Y.). Bed nets are the gold standard of global health interventions.

79
00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:54.200
This beats the gold standard while assuming it will almost certainly fail.

80
00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:02.560
The math doesn't care about your pessimism. The political success probability point estimate is one percent, with a ninety-five

81
00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.000
percent confidence interval of zero point one percent to ten percent.

82
00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:15.500
The expected cost per D.A.L.Y. point estimate is zero point one seven seven dollars, with a ninety-five percent confidence

83
00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:21.020
interval of zero point zero two nine dollars to three dollars and twenty cents.

84
00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:28.760
The cost-effectiveness versus bed nets point estimate is five hundred and three times, with a ninety-five percent confidence

85
00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:35.420
interval of thirty times to three thousand times. The bed net cost per D.A.L.Y.

86
00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:42.200
point estimate is eighty-nine dollars, with a ninety-five percent confidence interval of seventy-eight dollars to one hundred

87
00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:51.460
dollars. Historical Precedents (The Depressing Table). The zero point seven percent official development assistance, or

88
00:10:51.460 --> 00:11:00.380
O.D.A., Target aimed for zero point seven percent of gross national income, or G.N.I., to be given as foreign aid; it was

89
00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:07.480
achieved by approximately twenty percent of countries over fifty-plus years, primarily Scandinavia and Germany.

90
00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:15.940
The Kyoto Protocol set binding emissions targets and saw about fifty-five percent compliance initially before collapsing

91
00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:20.460
between nineteen ninety-seven and twenty twelve, noting the U.S.

92
00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:28.800
never ratified and Canada withdrew. The Paris Agreement involves non-binding pledges with approximately fifteen to twenty-five

93
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:35.840
percent of nations currently on track since twenty fifteen; though one hundred and ninety-five countries signed, perhaps

94
00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:44.200
only forty actually meant it. The NATO two percent G.D.P. defense spending commitment was met by approximately seventy-two

95
00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:51.740
percent of members in twenty twenty-four and has been in place since twenty fourteen, though compliance was only about thirty-two

96
00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:57.040
percent until a land war in Europe motivated action. The E.U.

97
00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:04.540
Stability Pact aimed for a three percent deficit and sixty percent debt limit but has been routinely violated since nineteen

98
00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.860
ninety-seven, with France and Germany breaking their own rules.

99
00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.980
Pattern: International financial commitments achieve less than twenty-five percent meaningful compliance.

100
00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:22.560
Your species signs things and then doesn't do them. It's like a New Year's resolution, but for geopolitics.

101
00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:32.060
The gym membership analogy is overused but accurate: purchased with enthusiasm in January, abandoned by February, cited

102
00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:38.980
in arguments about personal growth throughout March. Why a one percent Treaty is Harder Than Most Precedents.

103
00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:45.760
Let's be honest about the obstacles. This is not a chapter written by someone trying to sell you something.

104
00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:53.280
(It is. But at least it's honest about the hard parts.). It touches military budgets, and politicians would rather discuss

105
00:12:53.280 --> 00:13:00.980
their browser history than their defense budget. It requires ongoing annual allocation, not a one-time signature like banning

106
00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:08.960
a weapon, which makes it a gym membership for peace: monthly, forever, with no option to cancel by simply not going.

107
00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:16.340
There's no immediate security threat driving it, unlike NATO spending increases post-Ukraine; hard to motivate governments

108
00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:23.700
without an active catastrophe, even though one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day apparently doesn't qualify as one

109
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:31.900
(it doesn't qualify because the deaths are distributed, quiet, and polite, and your species only responds to deaths that

110
00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:40.900
are concentrated, loud, and rude). The mechanism is novel: military-to-health reallocation has never been done, and humans

111
00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:48.200
are suspicious of things they haven't tried before, which is odd for a species that also invented bungee jumping, cryptocurrency,

112
00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:56.000
and putting pineapple on pizza. And it's a coordination problem: benefits require critical mass, nobody wants to go first,

113
00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:02.390
and everybody wants to go second, which is a fine strategy for a conga line but not for treaty negotiation.

114
00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:11.320
Why a one percent Treaty Might Beat the Odds. It's self-funding: the health dividends exceed the "cost," so you make money

115
00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:19.000
doing this, which is unusual for moral obligations and also the entire reason it might work, because your species does not

116
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.800
do things for moral reasons but does do things for money reasons.

117
00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:30.760
It has bipartisan appeal: "Should we cure more diseases?" polls well across every political spectrum, which is one of the

118
00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:36.020
only questions that does, alongside "Is water wet?" and "Should we pay Congress less?"

119
00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:43.820
It has a referendum pathway that can bypass government resistance through direct democracy, which politicians hate, which

120
00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:51.540
is how you know it works. It has visible beneficiaries: one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day, which is hard to

121
00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:58.760
argue with even for people who argue professionally. And it has network effects: each signing country makes the next more

122
00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:06.580
likely, because peer pressure is humanity's most reliable technology, older than fire, more consistent than gravity, and

123
00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:10.000
the reason your teenagers wear the same shoes. Probability Estimates.

124
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:17.660
Economists would find the following defensible (and economists find very little defensible, which is their brand): Scenario:

125
00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:25.020
Pessimistic floor. Probability: zero point one percent. Translation: Everything goes wrong.

126
00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:31.560
Global crises. Political gridlock. Asteroid, possibly. Scenario: Conservative central.

127
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:37.100
Probability: one percent. Translation: ninety-nine percent chance of failure.

128
00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:43.280
Still five hundred and three times better than bed nets. Scenario: Optimistic ceiling.

129
00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:49.080
Probability: twenty-five percent. Translation: A major crisis opens a political window.

130
00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:56.820
Or humans just... decide to be smart for once. The model uses: Central estimate one percent.

131
00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:04.580
Expected Value (Even When You Assume Failure). At one percent probability of success, expected R.O.I.

132
00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:11.220
is eight hundred and forty-eight thousand to one (still positive, even assuming near-certain failure) and expected cost

133
00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:18.340
per disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, is seventeen point seven cents, which is five hundred and three times

134
00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:24.600
better than bed nets at eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y. Downside: capped at around one billion dollars.

135
00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.080
You lose the cost of a nice yacht. Upside: unbounded. You cure most diseases.

136
00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:36.700
Even assuming a ninety-nine percent chance of total failure, this beats the gold standard of global health interventions.

137
00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:43.780
The asymmetry is the point. The downside is a yacht. The upside is the end of most disease.

138
00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:51.020
Your species makes bets with worse odds every day in Las Vegas, except in Las Vegas the upside is a slightly larger pile

139
00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:54.680
of papers and the downside is a slightly smaller pile of papers.

140
00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:01.300
Here the upside is civilization-altering and the downside is one fewer yacht in the marina.

141
00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:10.579
The yacht will be fine. It was going to depreciate anyway. Why The Treaty Won't Stop at one percent Most international commitments

142
00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:18.240
stagnate after signing. You saw the depressing table. Zero point seven percent official development assistance, or O.D.A.,

143
00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:22.859
targets unmet for fifty plus years. Kyoto abandoned. Paris missed.

144
00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:26.220
Your track record with follow-through makes goldfish look disciplined.

145
00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:33.860
So why would a one percent health treaty be any different? Because this time, people get paid.

146
00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:40.780
The eighty-ten-ten funding structure allocates ten percent of treaty funding (two point seven two billion dollars per year

147
00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:48.100
at one percent) to Incentive Alignment Bonds. These are securities that pay out when treaties *expand*, not just maintain.

148
00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:54.660
People get rich when more diseases get cured. Finally, a financial instrument that isn't morally bankrupt.

149
00:17:55.320 --> 00:18:01.120
It only took your species four hundred years of financial instruments to produce one that doesn't require someone to suffer.

150
00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:08.340
Progress. The Expansion Mechanism. At a one percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B.

151
00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:15.720
pool is two point seven two billion dollars, creating strong political pressure, comparable to ten times the largest N.G.O.

152
00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:22.500
budgets. At a two percent treaty level, the pool is five point four four billion dollars, creating very strong political

153
00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:29.780
pressure at a nation-state level. At a five percent treaty level, the pool is thirteen point six billion dollars, creating

154
00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:33.300
overwhelming pressure equivalent to a major industry lobby.

155
00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:40.640
At a ten percent treaty level, the pool is twenty-seven point two billion dollars, which is probably irresistible and has

156
00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:45.640
no historical precedent. Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty.

157
00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:52.040
Zero point seven percent O.D.A. failed because nobody made money when countries complied.

158
00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:57.900
The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance have small budgets, compete with each other, and run on guilt.

159
00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.950
Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency. Guilt gets you to the gym in January.

160
00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:07.320
Greed gets you there in July. Guilt volunteers at the soup kitchen once.

161
00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:12.880
Greed builds the soup kitchen and names it after itself. Guilt is what your species feels.

162
00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:19.040
Greed is what your species does. This treaty runs on what your species does.

163
00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.900
The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

164
00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:28.210
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent, then ten percent.

165
00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:35.580
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

166
00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:42.860
They have Bloomberg terminals. They have lobbyists. They have the same tools that defense contractors currently use to expand

167
00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:45.960
military budgets, except pointed in the opposite direction.

168
00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:55.740
The Ratchet Effect. Once one percent passes, two point seven two billion dollars a year flows to Incentive Alignment Bond,

169
00:19:55.780 --> 00:20:03.140
or I.A.B. holders pushing for two percent. When two percent passes, five point four four billion dollars a year pushes for

170
00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:09.340
five percent. When five percent passes, thirteen point six billion dollars a year pushes for ten percent.

171
00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:17.040
The political pressure for expansion accelerates with each success, because each expansion funds a larger lobbying force

172
00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:23.060
for the next expansion, because the people who got rich at one percent want to get richer at two percent, and the people

173
00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:29.980
who got rich at two percent want to get richer at five percent, and so on until the entire engine runs on compound greed,

174
00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:37.520
which is like compound interest but for not dying. And the politicians receiving that pressure have their own compound greed.

175
00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:45.140
The treaty's ten percent political incentive allocation scales identically: at one percent, it's two point seven two billion

176
00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:51.080
dollars a year funding campaign support and post-office career rewards for legislators who voted yes.

177
00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:59.100
At five percent, five times that. A politician who votes to expand the treaty is voting to expand their own reelection budget.

178
00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:02.860
A politician who votes against expansion is voting to shrink it.

179
00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:08.430
You didn't change what politicians are. You changed what they're rewarded for.

180
00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:13.620
Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned by February.

181
00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:20.140
I.A.B.s are the opposite: the constituency for expansion *grows* with each success.

182
00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:26.910
Greed doesn't take holidays. Greed doesn't forget. Greed doesn't lose interest when a new television show comes on.

183
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:31.320
Greed is the only human motivation that has never once failed to show up.

184
00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:36.450
This treaty runs on it. On Wishonia, we find this arrangement embarrassing.

185
00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:41.840
On Earth, it's the only arrangement that works. Long-Term Feasibility.

186
00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:47.840
With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political engine.

187
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:52.860
A machine that runs on greed and outputs health. Embarrassing, but effective.

188
00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.280
The most effective things your species has ever built have all been embarrassing.

189
00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:01.800
The internet was built for sharing research papers and is used for arguing with strangers.

